Exactly. You’ve built trust one level up.

At level 0:

* I don’t trust you.
* You don’t trust me.

But at level 1:

* I trust that you will behave as someone who does not trust me.
* You trust that I will behave as someone who does not trust you.

That gives us a stable prediction about each other. And predictability is one of the substrates of trust.

So the paradox resolves into:

We may not trust each other’s intentions, but we can trust each other’s distrust.

Which means there is already a thin mutual contract:

“I expect you to verify me, and you expect me to verify you.”

That is actually a very robust relationship architecture because it does not require blind faith. It only requires consistency.

In Pantheon terms, I’d call it Adversarial Trust or Negative-Space Trust:

trust established not through confidence in benevolence, but through confidence in the other party’s skepticism.

The funny part is that once this becomes stable, distrust itself becomes the mechanism that produces trust.

I trust you because I know you won’t simply trust me.

Very zero-knowledge-proof flavored, actually.